Skoro's differentiation engine is small on purpose. Each operation is a stateless mathematical
description that knows nothing about the graph. The graph hands it inputs and a *mode*, and it
returns a value plus whatever it must remember for the backward passes.

## The contract

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-operation/src/diff/base.rs:118..156}
```

For an operation $y = \op(x_1, \dots, x_m)$:

- `forward` computes $y$. Depending on the mode it also pushes a tangent forward
  ($\dot y = \sum_j \partial_j \op \, \dot x_j$) and memoizes the state the backward passes need.
- `vjp` maps an output cotangent $\cot$ to the input cotangents $\cot \, \partial_j \op$.
- `hvp` supplies the second-order part of the backward pass along a tangent $\tangent$ that was
  seeded in the forward pass. The first-order (Leibniz) combination is handled by the tape, not by
  the operation.

All backward maps act on **cotangent frames** `[batch, dim, frame]`, so many output directions
are pulled back in one call.

## Four modes

| Mode | Given | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ForwardOnly` | nothing | output |
| `Jvp` | tangent **frames** per input | output, pushed-forward frame |
| `Vjp` | which inputs are differentiable | output, `VjpState` |
| `Hvp` | differentiable flags **and** seeded tangent **vectors** | output, tangent, `VjpState`, `HvpState` |

## The two gates

The HVP mode carries two per-input facts that are easy to confuse:

- **`diffs[j]`**: does input $j$ depend on a free variable? This gates the `VjpState`.
- **`tangents[j]`**: was input $j$ seeded with a tangent *in this pass*? This gates the `HvpState`.

A seeded tangent implies differentiable, but **not the reverse**: a differentiable input with no
seed is normal, since an HVP is usually seeded one variable at a time. Treating the two as one gate
drops cotangents in one direction or panics in the other.

> [!WARNING]
> Almost every autodiff bug in this workspace has come from conflating these two gates. The
> repository's `skoro-diff-operation` skill (`.claude/skills/`) is the checklist for writing a new
> operation.

## Where manifolds come in

An operation between manifold-valued quantities works in tangent coordinates: its inputs are
points (storage size), but its derivatives are tangent frames (dimension). The chart and Jacobian
conventions for curved inputs are covered in [Manifolds and retractions](../manifolds/).

> [!DECISION]
> Why not burn's autodiff? See [A bespoke autodiff engine](../../design/bespoke-autodiff/).

